@page "/learn/label-and-input-ids"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Labels and input ids · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Labels and input ids</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Clicking a label should put the cursor in its field. Why does every form need hand-typed ids for that?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Any render mode · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a <code>&lt;Label For&gt;</code> component, and every built-in input now
        renders its own <code>id</code>. In .NET 10 inputs had no <code>id</code> and there was no
        <code>Label</code>. <code>[Display]</code> names aren't new: validation messages already used them.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>New supplier</i> form with two required fields, <b>Company name</b> and <b>Contact email</b>.
        The model names both with <code>[Display]</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The form labels its fields in two common .NET 10 ways. <b>Company name</b> types the same <code>id</code>
        twice, once in the label's <code>for</code> and once on the input. <b>Email</b> wraps its input, and its
        label text is typed by hand:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The .NET 10 form</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;label for="supplier-<span class="err">compnay</span>"&gt;Company name&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;InputText id="supplier-<span class="ok">company</span>" @@bind-Value="Input!.Company" /&gt;
@(new string(' ', 24))<span class="err">↑ "compnay" ≠ "company": the label points at nothing</span>

&lt;label&gt;<span class="err">Email</span> &lt;InputText @@bind-Value="Input!.Email" type="email" /&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
@(new string(' ', 7))<span class="err">↑ typed by hand: the model calls this field "Contact email"</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Look closely at the two ids: the label says <code>supplier-<b>compnay</b></code>, the input says
        <code>supplier-<b>company</b></code>. The typo is deliberate. A label finds its input only by an exact
        id match, so this one is linked to nothing. Nothing warns you: it builds, renders and looks fine.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The form, labelled by hand" Url="demo/labels/suppliers/new/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: ids and names typed by hand" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Click the words <b>Company name</b>. Nothing happens: the cursor doesn't go into the field, because
            the label's <code>for</code> has the typo. Screen readers can't name the field either. Now press
            <b>Save supplier</b> with the form empty. The label says <b>Email</b>, but its message says
            "Contact email".
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>
    <p>
        Clicking the <b>Email</b> label does put the cursor in its field, because the label wraps its input.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Every label is typed twice</h2>
    <p>In .NET 10, a label and its input are linked only by text you type:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What .NET 10 renders for that form</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;label for="supplier-<span class="err">compnay</span>"&gt;Company name&lt;/label&gt;   <span class="err">✗ no element has this id</span>
&lt;input id="supplier-<span class="ok">company</span>" name="Input.Company"&gt;   <span class="err">✗ no label points here</span>

&lt;label&gt;Email &lt;input name="Input.Email"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;   <span class="ok">✓ clicking "Email" focuses the input</span>
<span class="dim">The Contact email field is required.</span>      <span class="err">✗ says "Contact email", the label says "Email"</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;InputText&gt;</code> didn't render an <code>id</code>, so you had to type the same id on the label
        and the input. A typo still builds, with no warning, and the label links to nothing. Wrapping the input
        avoids the id, but you still type the label text. Messages use
        <code>[Display(Name = "Contact email")]</code> and the label doesn't, so the two drift apart.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Use Label For</h2>
    <p>Replace each hand-written label with <code>&lt;Label For&gt;</code>. The <code>&lt;InputText&gt;</code> lines stay the same.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;Label For="() =&gt; Input!.Company" /&gt;</span>
&lt;InputText @@bind-Value="Input!.Company" /&gt;

<span class="hl">&lt;Label For="() =&gt; Input!.Email" /&gt;</span>
&lt;InputText @@bind-Value="Input!.Email" type="email" /&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 every built-in input renders an <code>id</code> based on the field it's bound to, with dots
        turned into underscores. <code>Input.Company</code> becomes <code>Input_Company</code>. <code>Label</code>
        puts that same id in its <code>for</code> attribute, and takes its text from <code>[Display]</code>. Here's the
        same form using it.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same form, with Label For" Url="demo/labels/suppliers/new"
                Label=".NET 11: Label For">
        <Caption>
            Click the words <b>Company name</b>. The cursor goes into the field. Press <b>Save supplier</b> with the
            form empty: the label and the message both say "Contact email".
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the page behind Fig. 2, the model both forms share, and the page behind Fig. 1:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Labels/NewSupplier.razor" Feature="label-and-input-ids">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's form. Only the two <code>&lt;Label For&gt;</code> lines are new. The
            <code>&lt;InputText&gt;</code> lines are highlighted too, but the code is the same as in .NET 10. What
            changed is that they now render an <code>id</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Labels/NewSupplierForm.cs" Feature="label-and-input-ids">
        <Caption>
            The model behind both figures. Each field is named once. Labels and messages both use that name.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Labels/NewSupplierNet10.razor" Feature="label-and-input-ids">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's form: a typed <code>id</code> with a typo, and a wrapping label with the wrong text.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Type it twice, or no label</h2>
    <p>
        Fig. 1 shows two .NET 10 approaches, and both make you type something twice. Either pass an
        <code>id</code> to <code>&lt;InputText&gt;</code> and type it again in <code>for</code>, or wrap the input
        in a label and type the field's name again. They work only while the two copies match, and a typo, like
        the one in Fig. 1, breaks them with no warning. A third approach, a label next to the input with no <code>for</code>, doesn't work at all:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">A bare label in .NET 10</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;label&gt;Company name&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;InputText @@bind-Value="Input!.Company" /&gt;
<span class="dim">renders</span> &lt;input name="Input.Company"&gt;   <span class="err">no id: clicking focuses nothing, no accessible name</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;Label For&gt;</code> doesn't exist in .NET 10. It builds with warning RZ10012, "Found markup
        element with unexpected name 'Label'", and renders as an unknown <code>&lt;Label&gt;</code> element.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One name, nothing to type</h2>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;Label For&gt;</code> uses <code>[Display(Name)]</code>. If that's missing, it uses
        <code>[DisplayName]</code>, then the property name. Either way, the label and its messages use the same
        name, and there's no id to mistype. If you set your own id on an input, see Good to know.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Need your own id on an input?</b> Then put the input inside the label: <code>&lt;Label For="..."&gt;&lt;InputText id="..." ... /&gt;&lt;/Label&gt;</code>. A label that wraps its input doesn't need a <code>for</code>. With the input beside the label instead, it breaks: your <code>id</code> replaces the generated one, but <code>&lt;Label For&gt;</code> still writes <code>for="Input_Company"</code>, which then matches nothing.</li>
        <li><b>The id follows the property path.</b> A nested field is <code>Input_Address_City</code>. A field in a list is <code>Input_Contacts[0]_Name</code>; escape the <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> if you use it in CSS.</li>
        <li><b>Inputs now render an id.</b> Check your CSS, scripts and test selectors for clashes with the new ids.</li>
        <li><b>Your own inputs</b> built on <code>InputBase&lt;T&gt;</code> should render <code>id="@@IdAttributeValue"</code>, so labels can find them.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
